    {
      "id": "claim_56bde8ef033b40bcb1d7f864d22b96d3",
      "statement": "Smart Tutor uses Keycloak 26.3 as its identity provider with a single OIDC realm named 'smart-tutor', configured in local/keycloak/realm-smart-tutor.json and auto-imported on first start via the --import-realm flag.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://compose.yaml#L24-L38",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:9d30263d6cea29f244d9fdd48fac0faf2ac48c0877622309e15e2242a3afe32b:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://local/keycloak/realm-smart-tutor.json#L1-L8",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:41f196b2a91ac1136a415312518275be2fb6900d06bccfae0cc8a48056040179:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_175941b1f6dd484183a91136c7e10cec",
      "statement": "The smart-tutor realm has three clients: smart-tutor-web (public PKCE SPA client), smart-tutor-api (bearer-only resource server), and rag-mcp (bearer-only resource server). The two resource-server clients have standardFlowEnabled: false and never initiate flows.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://local/keycloak/realm-smart-tutor.json#L24-L63",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:ec3fa9af48d74355c9cde0019f93cf29261aef7a46d0595948a7702e4905f26f:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_140960c6e79a4bdbac292e72cd3d736a",
      "statement": "The smart-tutor-web client carries two audience protocol mappers that add 'smart-tutor-api' and 'rag-mcp' to the aud claim of every access token (access token only; not the ID token), so one token issued at login is valid for both resource servers.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://local/keycloak/realm-smart-tutor.json#L33-L47",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:017096cbf8248eca9430b5f2b4029a51160bc814c05f4bd8a16db0047821e59b:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_8a987a0583054d52917ea2bbaaf9955f",
      "statement": "The web app performs Authorization Code + PKCE (S256) against the smart-tutor realm; the resulting access token is sent to the API as a Bearer token, the API validates it and forwards the same token in runtime context to the agent, and the agent attaches it as an HTTP Authorization header on every MCP request — never in a prompt.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://local/keycloak/realm-smart-tutor.json#L29-L34",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:7f4ecccd1ae8633459af1697f748e83c1128b7e50242f3cd52febe618ed40d1f:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://sdd/prd.md#L326-L329",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:43aa2d01be48eafc6c849b10e413d2dbe56ac4a712c8b42d4738fe2260a4c43e:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_2f7f954ad69c4971b663aa75c918bea0",
      "statement": "JWT validation in the auth adapter checks: JWKS (fetched from the Keycloak issuer), iss (must equal keycloak_issuer from Settings), aud (smart-tutor-api for the API; rag-mcp for the MCP server), and exp. The verifier is currently a stub (M0 task).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://backend/src/smart_tutor/shared/adapters/auth/__init__.py#L1-L1",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:526272e6ed41cf58e31a6590a8ca92445619f0ff049b934efc72a6494db45fb1:"
